@topic stdlib.module.std::ascii
title: std::ascii -- standard namespace
summary: Structural ASCII classification of byte values: is_digit, is_upper, is_lower, is_alphabetic and is_alphanumeric over std::byte::Byte.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::ascii
keywords: ascii; character class; isdigit; isalpha; classify bytes
related: stdlib.std::byte::Byte stdlib.std::str::encode_utf8 stdlib.std::str::is_tcl_alpha

== DESCRIPTION
  std::ascii::is_digit(b)        '0'..'9'   (48..57)
  std::ascii::is_upper(b)        'A'..'Z'   (65..90)
  std::ascii::is_lower(b)        'a'..'z'   (97..122)
  std::ascii::is_alphabetic(b)   is_upper(b) or is_lower(b)
  std::ascii::is_alphanumeric(b) is_alphabetic(b) or is_digit(b)

Every predicate is total over Byte's own 256-value domain and locale-,
encoding- and platform-independent: no libc isdigit/isalpha/isalnum, no Tcl
character classes, no Unicode categories, no case folding, no UTF-8. A
high-bit byte (128..255) is simply not ASCII -- every predicate answers
false for it, never an error (a Byte is always a valid Byte; classifying it
is total).

Byte is the parameter type, never Int/UnicodeChar/str: this namespace
classifies byte *values*, not Unicode scalars or source characters (see
ASCII-AND-URI-UNRESERVED.md). The boundary constants below (48, 57, 65, 90,
97, 122) are ordinary Int literals compared directly against the Byte
parameter -- Byte is `Int in 0..255` (stdlib/byte.bot), so an Int-typed
comparison operand needs no conversion; wrapping every boundary in its own
runtime Byte(...) checked construction would add nothing but nominal
symmetry (see the report's "Boundary representation choice").

This namespace deliberately owns only ASCII *structure*: is_digit, is_upper,
is_lower, is_alphabetic, is_alphanumeric, and nothing else -- no
is_uri_unreserved, is_identifier_char, is_control, is_hex_digit, to_
upper/to_lower, case-insensitive/locale flags. URI-specific punctuation ('-'
'.' '_' '~') is policy, owned by web.bot's own is_unreserved, which composes
std::ascii::is_alphanumeric with its own retained punctuation set -- never
duplicated or folded into an ASCII class here.

The one dependency of this leaf module is the type of its parameters:
`import type std::byte::Byte` (stdlib/byte.bot's `type Byte = Int in
0..255`) binds the short type name Byte in this file, and no other name of
namespace byte (IMPORTS.md). Every consumer of `ascii::` that needs to
*construct* a Byte already names the byte namespace itself.

