== NOTES
`import std::binary` for the ten intrinsics and the scalar field types
std::binary::U8 ... std::binary::I64. A `layout` declaration names a fixed
number of bytes with explicit size, alignment and field offsets; every
multi-byte field is little-endian (the target's order), and padding is never
a field. The layout is static: it is named at a parse or a query and read
from the value's type at an emit, never a runtime value. A field may be a
fixed array, `field name: array 16 of std::ffi::c::Char at 8`: N elements of
any representation (a scalar, a pointer, a nested layout, another array),
element i at the field's offset plus i times the element's width; its
copied value is a List of exactly N elements (FIXED-LAYOUT-ARRAYS.md).
There are no bitfields, variable-length tails or zero-length arrays in a
layout; overlapping interpretations are a union layout's (LAYOUT-UNIONS.md).

A field may be C's float or double (std::ffi::c::Float, 4 bytes;
std::ffi::c::Double, 8), its value a Float64. A read of one --
std::binary::parse, std::binary::read, std::binary::load,
std::binary::element, a view's field -- fails with the declared
NonFiniteFloat when its bits are a NaN or an infinity (negative zero reads
as 0.0); a write, store or emit of a float rounds the Float64 to nearest,
ties to even, and fails with NonFiniteFloat when it overflows the float,
before a byte is written. A call is charged NonFiniteFloat only when it
reads a float or a double or writes a float: the errors listed below include
it for those calls alone (concept.c-floats).

A field may be a C pointer (std::ffi::c::Ptr[T] or std::ffi::c::FnPtr[F], of
the target's pointer width). Such a layout is native-only: bytes never
become a pointer, so std::binary::parse and std::binary::emit of it are
LAYOUT-NATIVE-ONLY, and it is read from live native memory through a
NativeView[L] (std::binary::native::view, then its fields or
std::binary::load).

Writes name their field statically, LAYOUT.FIELD or a path through nested
layouts (Header.origin.x). A record in byte storage the program owns is
filled a field at a time: std::binary::write(data, L.f, v) is a new
std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes with that field written (data unchanged), and
a pointer field takes an existing pointer only through the explicit
std::binary::write_foreign. Live native memory foreign code handed out is
written through a NativeEdit[L] (std::binary::native::edit, then
std::binary::store). NATIVE-LAYOUT-WRITES.md.

A fixed array read through a view or an edit (v.items) is a live array,
NativeView[array N of E] or NativeEdit[array N of E]:
std::binary::element(a, i) reads one element at a dynamic index
(IndexNotFound outside 0..N-1, no failure when the index is proven),
std::binary::store_element(e.items, i, v) writes one scalar or pointer
element, and std::binary::load(a) copies the whole array into its List. A
designator names an element statically too (L.items[3], L.m[1][2],
L.pts[2].x), and a whole array is written from a List of exactly its extent
(the declared LayoutExtentMismatch otherwise, before anything is written).

