related: concept.integer-domains stdlib.bit_and stdlib.module.std::ascii

== DESCRIPTION
Every function here is ordinary Botlish with a verified refined result
primitives core/scalarbits.tcl registers as root natives (bit_and, bit_or,
bit_xor, shift_left, shift_right -- already in native/lower.tcl's own op
table, so each compiles inline wherever it is called, module function or
not). This is deliberately NOT a thin wrapper around a separate native with
a declared HighNibble/LowNibble/Nibble result type: that shape was tried
first and does not natively compile from a call site *inside* a cross-file
module function (which is exactly this file's own shape, and web.bot's
high_nibble's) -- native lowering's -native-body/-module-fn bridge
(native/native.tcl) is a syntactic pre-pass over only the entry program's
own raw core IR, so it can substitute a native called directly from an .ir
program's top level, but never one called from inside a .bot module's own
compiled body (core/scalarbits.tcl's header has the full trace). Plain
functions over already-lowerable primitives sidestep that wall entirely.
Optional result contracts now preserve each nominal type across ordinary
module calls; hir/range.tcl derives interval and exact-set facts from that
semantic type without reanalyzing this body at call sites.

  std::byte::high_nibble(b)   position-preserving high-half extraction: b&0xF0
  std::byte::low_nibble(b)    position-preserving low-half extraction: b&0x0F
  std::byte::nibble(x)        positioned nibble (either half) -> logical Nibble
                         (0..15): ((x&0x0F)|(x>>4))&0x0F -- correct for
                         any x in Byte's own domain (0..255): recovers x
                         unchanged when x is already low-positioned
                         (x<=15, so x>>4=0) and x>>4 when x is high-
                         positioned (x's low 4 bits are 0 by construction,
                         so x&0x0F=0) -- one branch-free formula, no
                         special-casing of which half x came from.
  std::byte::complement(b)    one's complement over Byte's 8-bit domain: b^0xFF
                         (never Int's own arbitrary-precision complement).
  std::byte::position_low(n)  Nibble -> LowNibble: representation-preserving.
  std::byte::position_high(n) Nibble -> HighNibble: n<<4, positioned.
  std::byte::from_int(value: int)  Int -> Byte, errors BelowRange, AboveRange:
                         the ordinary explicitly-fallible Int -> Byte
                         conversion (EXPLICIT-ERROR-COMPLETIONS.md;
                         replaces the old callable-type-syntax Byte(x),
                         removed from source -- see that report for why).
                         A statically provable in-range/out-of-range
                         argument needs no handler, or is a compile-time
                         KNOWN-ERROR, respectively -- see
                         STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md.
  std::byte::set(chars)       List[UnicodeChar] -> ImmutableSet[Byte], errors
                         AboveRange: each char's own Unicode scalar value
                         (std::char::scalar_value), converted via from_int above
                         -- never UTF-8 encoding, so 'é' (U+00E9) becomes
                         the one Byte 233, not the two UTF-8 bytes 0xC3
                         0xA9 -- see BYTE-SET.md. Propagates from_int's
                         own declared error for any char outside Byte's
                         domain (e.g. 'Ā', U+0100, or any astral
                         character): no partial set. BelowRange is not
                         declared: std::char::scalar_value's own result is always
                         nonnegative, so from_int's BelowRange branch is
                         statically proven unreachable from here, generically
                         (STATIC-COMPLETION-PROOFS.md), not by a Byte-
                         specific rule.

ASCII/encoding interpretation, `is_unreserved`, and URI grammar are
deliberately absent: `byte::` holds only operations intrinsic to byte/bit
representation (core/scalarbits.tcl's header, and the milestone's own
namespace discipline -- see BYTE-NIBBLE-BIT-ARITHMETIC.md).

