@topic stdlib.module.std::darwin::io
title: std::darwin::io -- standard namespace
summary: The process's standard I/O on macOS as one execution-environment context (CONTEXTS.md.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::darwin::io

== DESCRIPTION
  with context std::darwin::io::create()  install it (entry program)
  std::darwin::io::write(data)            write(2) to the context's stdout
  std::darwin::io::write_error(data)      write(2) to the context's stderr
  std::darwin::io::read(buffer)           read(2) from the context's stdin

DarwinIO is std::linux::io::LinuxIO's macOS sibling: ONE context, the
process I/O environment, holding the three standard descriptors. A function
that uses it says so once (`context io: DarwinIO`) and is called with its
ordinary arguments; code between the entry program and it declares nothing
(the compiler derives the requirement), and a call before `with context
std::darwin::io::create()` is MISSING-CONTEXT at compile time.

Capability boundary (DIRECT-MODULES.md). `darwin` is a direct module: its
write and read take any descriptor. This module is not: every function here
that calls into `darwin` requires DarwinIO, the context it defines itself,
so the raw authority reaches its callers only as that capability -- and
nothing above std::darwin::io is direct for using it. Every external effect
flows

  std::darwin::io::write --> std::darwin::write --> libSystem's write(3)

through ordinary C calls (C-FFI.md): no syscall, no compiler knowledge of
I/O. The low-level layer stays as it is: code that wants an explicit
descriptor calls std::darwin::write(fd, data) / std::darwin::read(fd,
buffer).

Representation authority. FileDescriptor and DarwinIO are opaque
(OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md): only this module may construct them or look inside; a
DarwinIO a function receives as its context can be held and passed but not
taken apart. The descriptor token is a std::ffi::c::Int, the C int every
libSystem descriptor is; create() mints the three standard ones, 0, 1 and 2.
The token does not claim the descriptor is open or stays open.

DarwinIO implements the portable std::io::IO context trait
(CONTEXT-TRAITS.md) with write_text and write_error_text below: ordinary
functions of this module whose one context parameter is a DarwinIO and whose
signatures match std::io::IO's requirements. Nothing registers them: where a
call needs std::io::IO and DarwinIO is the one installed context that
implements it, the compiler binds `io.write_text(...)` to a direct call of
std::darwin::io::write_text -- no dispatch, no hidden argument, no provider
table at run time.

The completion policy is LinuxIO's, so std::io::IO means the same on both
platforms: all of the text, or std::io::WriteFailed. A short positive write
is completed by writing the rest; a failed write (-1, whatever the errno --
EINTR and EAGAIN included: no retry) and a write that makes no progress are
std::io::WriteFailed; no count, descriptor or errno escapes.

Not here (deliberately): errno mapping beyond std::io::WriteFailed (the raw
std::darwin::write/read keep it), buffering, text input, close/ownership
