@topic stdlib.module.std::ffi::c
title: std::ffi::c -- standard namespace
summary: The C ABI value universe: target-sized C integer types, and affine C pointers with null, null?, clone, erase and retype.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::ffi::c
keywords: c types; c pointers; ffi; foreign function interface; c abi
related: concept.c-ffi concept.affine

== DESCRIPTION
These are the values a C boundary carries, modeled as C's ABI sees them, not
as C's source language spells them. Nothing here calls C yet: there is no
`extern c fn`, no linking and no callback (C-ABI-TYPES.md, "What is not
here").

C integer scalars (intrinsic types of this namespace):

  std::ffi::c::Char std::ffi::c::SChar std::ffi::c::UChar std::ffi::c::Short std::ffi::c::UShort
  std::ffi::c::Int std::ffi::c::UInt std::ffi::c::Long std::ffi::c::ULong
  std::ffi::c::LongLong std::ffi::c::ULongLong

Each is an integer domain: its values are ordinary Ints in exactly the
compilation's C target's range of that C type (`-target`, the host's by
default: std::ffi::c::Long is Int in -2^63..2^63-1 on linux-x86_64 and
-2^31..2^31-1 on windows-x86_64; std::ffi::c::Char is signed or unsigned as
the target's plain char is). Arithmetic on them is Botlish arithmetic (no C
promotion, no wrapping); an Int becomes one only where its range proves it
fits. std::ffi::c::Int and std::ffi::c::Long stay two types where their
ranges agree.

C floating types (intrinsic types of this namespace,
C-FLOAT-FFI-MILESTONE-1.md): C representations whose values are Float64s.

  std::ffi::c::Double       C's double, IEEE 754 binary64 (8 bytes): every
                            Float64 is one, exactly
  std::ffi::c::Float        C's float, IEEE 754 binary32 (4 bytes): a
                            Float64 leaving for one is rounded to nearest,
                            ties to even, and one that rounds to an
                            infinity is NonFiniteFloat before C runs (never
                            clamped); a float entering widens exactly

They name C types only -- an extern c fn's parameters and result, a function
pointer's signature, a pointee, a layout field -- and stay two types
(Ptr[std::ffi::c::Float] is no Ptr[std::ffi::c::Double]). A float or double
entering Botlish is a Float64 only when it is finite: a NaN or an infinity
is NonFiniteFloat (the call, or the layout read, declares it), and negative
zero is zero. Where a value type is written, std::ffi::c::Double means
Float64 and std::ffi::c::Float is an error: Botlish has no binary32 number.
C long double has no type.

Pointers (intrinsic types and operations of this namespace):

  std::ffi::c::Void         the C void: an incomplete pointee, no values
  std::ffi::c::Ptr[T]       a C data pointer whose pointee identity is T:
                            std::ffi::c::Void, a C integer scalar, std::ffi::c::Bool,
                            std::ffi::c::Float or std::ffi::c::Double, an opaque
                            struct (an incomplete C tag's identity), or
                            another pointer
  std::ffi::c::FnPtr[Fn{args: [A, ...], return: R}]
                            a C function pointer (A and R C ABI types, R
                            unit for a void result)

  std::ffi::c::null[T]()         Ptr[T]      a null pointer
  std::ffi::c::null?(p)          bool        whether p is null (p.null?())
  std::ffi::c::clone(p)          p's type    a second alias of p (p.clone())
  std::ffi::c::erase(p)          Ptr[Void]   the same address as void *
  std::ffi::c::retype[T](p)      Ptr[T]      the same address of a Ptr[Void]

A pointer is affine: binding, passing, returning or storing it moves it, and
`p.clone()` is the one way to have two aliases. Affinity is not ownership: a
pointer owns, frees and retains nothing, and is never dereferenced here. It
is not an Int: no arithmetic, ordering, equality or integer conversion
exists, in either direction.

Temporary byte exposure (C-POINTER-BORROWING.md). A C pointer parameter of a
foreign call may admit existing Botlish byte storage for that one call --
never as a pointer value -- when the call's contract says, for that
parameter, that C does not keep the pointer (noescape) and whether C reads
or writes through it, and its pointee is a byte-sized C type
(std::ffi::c::Char, SChar, UChar) or std::ffi::c::Void:

  std::abi::bytes::Bytes         read access only: C sees the bytes
  std::abi::bytes::MutableBytes  read: its bytes as they are; write: a
                                 private copy C may modify, which the call
                                 returns as the parameter's updated value
                                 (the caller's own value never changes: a
                                 MutableBytes is a value)

A parameter that may keep the pointer, or about which nothing is known,
never admits it: `const` is not noescape, and unknown is not "probably
synchronous". No operation of this namespace (or any other) makes a Ptr of
byte storage, so no pointer into Botlish storage can be stored, returned,
