keywords: check length; validate length; exact length; assert length; fixed length; LengthMismatch
related: stdlib.module.std::list concept.list-length concept.scalar-refinements stdlib.std::list::length stdlib.std::decimal_rounded::round rule.unhandled-errors

== SIGNATURE
    std::list::with_length(list) -> list

== ERRORS
    LengthMismatch

A call must handle each (on NAME:), admit it in its function's errors
clause, or be proven unable to raise it (rule.unhandled-errors).

== USE
An intrinsic of namespace std::list: import std::list, then write
std::list::with_length. Method syntax: receiver.with_length(...) is the
same call.

== BACKENDS
every backend (interp, compile, cranelift-generic, cranelift)

== NOTES
The explicit way to mint a List length refinement (concept.list-length)
from a List whose length is not known statically. It compares the length
once and returns its argument unchanged -- no copy, no new List -- with the
type of its imprint; any other length completes with the builtin declared
error LengthMismatch. The imprint is explicit and must be a refined List
type (std::list::with_length[List[int]](xs) is a TYPE error, and without [T]
it is IMPRINT); the argument must already be a List of the imprint's
element type, since the check proves a length, never elements. Where a
guard reads better, `if xs.length() == N:` refines xs in its branch
without a call. Runs on every backend.

== EXAMPLES
@example accept
import std::list

fn triple(xs: List[int]) -> List[int]{length: 3} errors LengthMismatch:
    std::list::with_length[List[int]{length: 3}](xs)

fn middle(xs: List[int]) -> int:
    t = xs.triple():
        on LengthMismatch:
            return -1
    t.at(1)

[middle([4, 5, 6]), middle([4, 5])]
@value [5, -1]
@end

@topic stdlib.module.std::list
title: std::list -- standard namespace
summary: Immutable List operations: length, indexed access (at, get), append, and the search helpers any?, all?, none? and find.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::list
keywords: list functions; array functions; sequence operations
related: concept.lists concept.list-length task.list-element capability.map-filter

== DESCRIPTION
The namespace's intrinsics are root natives registered under their qualified
names -- std::list::length, std::list::at, std::list::append
(core/lists.tcl) -- and this module may never define a member of the same
name (surface/modules.tcl, DUPLICATE-NATIVE; STDLIB-NAMESPACES.md).
Everything below is ordinary Botlish over them:

  std::list::get(xs, i, default)   the element at i, or DEFAULT when i does
                               not designate an element: exactly
                               std::list::at(xs, i) with its IndexNotFound --
                               and nothing else -- handled by returning
                               DEFAULT. DEFAULT is an ordinary argument,
                               evaluated before the call like any other
                               (no lazy default); the result is never an
                               optional/null value. Every other failure of
                               std::list::at (a non-List or non-Int argument)
                               propagates unchanged.

and the search/reduction idioms built directly on the returning iterable
loop (`loop x in xs:`, RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md), Botlish's fundamental
collection-transformation primitive. Deliberately no map/filter/filter_map:
the returning loop already subsumes them (`loop x in xs: if not
predicate(x): continue \n x` is the filtering idiom; the search/reduction
helpers below are exactly the case where a genuine callback still pays
rent).

  std::list::any?(xs, predicate)   true iff some element satisfies predicate,
                               left to right, short-circuiting at the
                               first true. any?([], predicate) -> false.
  std::list::all?(xs, predicate)   true iff every element satisfies predicate,
                               left to right, short-circuiting at the
                               first false. all?([], predicate) -> true.
  std::list::none?(xs, predicate)  true iff no element satisfies predicate,
                               left to right, short-circuiting at the
                               first true. none?([], predicate) -> true.
                               Written directly (not merely "not any?(...)
                               "): a genuinely independent higher-order
                               idiom, and another honest predicate call
                               site for the next milestone's callvalue
                               corpus -- not special-cased against any?.
  std::list::find(xs, predicate)   the first element satisfying predicate,
                               left to right, short-circuiting at the
                               first match. No match (including an empty
                               xs) fails the checked NotFound error below
                               -- never null, undefined, or a sentinel.

Each function's own returning-loop result is deliberately unused: every body
short-circuits via `return` (or, for find, `fail`) before the loop would
ever exhaust normally with a meaningful collected List. The native backend's
discarded-result lowering (RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md items 20-22, 28-29)
applies at every one of these call sites, so none of these four helpers
causes an output-List allocation the caller never asked for -- see that
report's allocation census.

No `continue` appears below, deliberately: it would only ever change what a
*discarded* iteration contributes to a List none of these four functions
ever reads (a skipped iteration's own `if`, with no `else` and nothing after
it, already completes normally with `unit` -- ordinary Botlish
`if`-without-`else` typing, ASCII-AND-URI-UNRESERVED.md's own `if not
label_char?(...): return false` shape among many others -- so omitting
`continue` here costs nothing observable and keeps the shape of each
function to its one real decision). A `continue` remains load- bearing
wherever a returning loop's own collected List *is* read -- see item 4's own
filtering idiom, `loop x in xs: if not predicate(x): continue \n x`, in
RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md.

`predicate(x)` is an ordinary dynamic call through an untyped parameter
(`callvalue` at the interpreter/native level, exactly like stdlib/web.bot's
own `scan_while`'s `predicate(char_at(i))`): this milestone intentionally
does not add finite callable-target provenance, devirtualization, or any
other optimization of that call -- see the report's higher-order callvalue
census, and RETURNING-ITERABLE-LOOPS.md items 47/81.

== SOURCE
stdlib/list.bot (import std::list).

== MEMBERS
    std::list::NotFound            error
    std::list::all?                function
    std::list::any?                function
    std::list::append              intrinsic -- A new List of the elements ...
    std::list::at                  intrinsic -- The element of a List at in...
    std::list::find                function
    std::list::get                 function
    std::list::length              intrinsic -- The number of elements of a...
    std::list::none?               function
    std::list::with_length         intrinsic -- std::list::with_length[List...

Each member is a topic: -help NAME (e.g. -help std::list::NotFound).

== NOTES Four intrinsics (std::list::length, std::list::at,
std::list::append and the length validator std::list::with_length) and the
ordinary Botlish functions of stdlib/list.bot built on them. `import
std::list` makes both callable, qualified (std::list::at(xs, 0)) or as
methods (xs.at(0)). A List is immutable: append returns a new List and
nothing changes one in place (a MutableVector is the growable mutable
alternative).

There is deliberately no map, filter, reduce, sum, reverse, sort, slice,
concatenation of two Lists or index-of: a collecting loop
(`loop x in xs: f(x)`, with `continue` to skip) is the transformation
(capability.map-filter), and std::list::any?, all?, none? and find cover
search.

