@topic stdlib.module.std::windows::io
title: std::windows::io -- standard namespace
summary: The process's standard output and error on Windows as one execution-environment context (CONTEXTS.md.
index: stdlib
symbol: std::windows::io

== DESCRIPTION
  with context std::windows::io::create()  install it (entry program)
  std::windows::io::write(data)            WriteFile to the context's
                                           standard output: a
                                           std::windows::WriteResult
  std::windows::io::write_error(data)      the same to its standard error

WindowsIO is std::linux::io::LinuxIO's and std::darwin::io::DarwinIO's
Windows sibling: ONE context, the process's output environment. A function
that uses it says so once (`context io: WindowsIO`) and is called with its
ordinary arguments; code between the entry program and it declares nothing
(the compiler derives the requirement), and a call before `with context
std::windows::io::create()` is MISSING-CONTEXT at compile time.

What the context holds is the process's standard *devices* -- its output and
its error -- never a handle: a Windows standard handle is the process's
slot, read with GetStdHandle when a write needs it (windows::
standard_handle), and a C pointer cannot live in a context anyway (an
installed context is erased: AFFINE-ERASURE-UNSUPPORTED, C-ABI-TYPES.md). So
every write uses the handle the process has at that moment -- a console, a
file or a pipe, whatever the launcher redirected it to -- and the context
owns nothing: no handle is opened, duplicated or closed here.

Representation authority. WindowsIO is opaque (OPAQUE-STRUCTS.md): only this
module may construct it or look inside; a WindowsIO a function receives as
its context can be held and passed but not taken apart.

WindowsIO implements the portable std::io::IO context trait
(CONTEXT-TRAITS.md) with write_text and write_error_text below: ordinary
functions of this module whose one context parameter is a WindowsIO and
whose signatures match std::io::IO's requirements. Nothing registers them:
where a call needs std::io::IO and WindowsIO is the one installed context
that implements it, the compiler binds `io.write_text(...)` to a direct call
of std::windows::io:: write_text -- no dispatch, no hidden argument, no
provider table at run time.

The completion policy is LinuxIO's and DarwinIO's, so std::io::IO means the
same on every platform: all of the text, or std::io::WriteFailed. A short
write is completed by writing the rest; a write WriteFile reports failed
(whatever its last-error code: no retry), one that makes no progress, and a
device the process has no handle for (GetStdHandle's NULL) are
std::io::WriteFailed; no count, handle or error code escapes. The text is
written as its UTF-8 bytes, exactly: no UTF-16 conversion, no console code
page applied, no newline translation (a redirected output receives the
bytes; a console shows them through its output code page, which
WINDOWS-X86-64-MILESTONE-2.md's "Text and encoding" describes).

Not here (deliberately): input, files, the error code beyond
std::io::WriteFailed (the raw std::windows::write_file and
std::windows::io::write keep it), console modes, WriteConsoleW, buffering.

